    "chunk_text": "at regular test centres. Instead, VET officers conduct inspections within military camps. Many military camps have their own testing machines. So, what we do is, we send VET officers to examine the testing.\n\n\uf0b7 In 2024, 4,565 Army vehicles were tested\n\nunder this arrangement.\n\nstudy for the next phase of vehicle testing, which will integrate a roadworthiness test from 01st of January, 2028.\n\nKey findings and recommendations include:\n\n\uf0b7 Testing Method: Current \u201cno-load\u201d tests do\n\nnot reflect the real-world conditions of vehicles. Introduction of \u201cloaded\u201d testing is recommended.\n\n\uf0b7 Misuse of Certificates: Some vehicle owners\n\nmake temporary adjustments solely to pass tests. Regulations should be updated to include garage accreditation and improved technician training.\n\n\uf0b7 Public Awareness: Awareness of the health\n\nrisks of air pollution remains low. Continuous awareness campaigns are required.\n\n\uf0b7 Fuel Quality: Existing tests cannot detect\n\nadulterated fuel. Penalty systems should be updated accordingly.\n\n\uf0b7 Enforcement: No spot-fine system exists for\n\nfailed vehicles or non-compliant contractors. Enforcement mechanisms must be strengthened.\n\nYear\n\nTotal Initial\n\nTest count\n\nTotal Rev-enue (LKR in Million)\n\nContrac-\n\ntor\u2019s fee (LKR in Million)\n\nVET Fund\n\n(LKR in Million)\n\nGov. Tax\n\n(LKR in Million)\n\n2020 5,238,823 3067.6 2582.2 258.2 227.2\n\n2021 5.550,473 3232.7 2721.1 272.1 239.5\n\n2022 5,573,865 3388 2722.3 273 393.4\n\n2023 5,720,021 4365.5 3364.7 336.5 664.3\n\n2024 5,799,683 4843.8 3638.5 363.3 841.5\n\n\uf0b7 Police Role: Current procedures are too\n\ncomplex, discouraging roadside checks. Simpler practices should be introduced by law. \uf0b7 Old Vehicles: The absence of a scrapping\n\npolicy allows very old vehicles to remain in use. Phased retirement is recommended. \uf0b7 Institutional Authority: The Vehicle Emission\n\nTesting (VET) Fund is the only body overseeing the programme. The VET Fund lacks legal standing. It should be strengthened and recognized by law to ensure proper oversight."
