    "md5": "f0d0ba3a1eaa8abae73fa302866947f7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1778,
    "chunk_text": "1. Will the Hon. Minister state what immediate remedial measures are being implemented to prevent refund delays spanning minimum 105 days or more, which are creating a crippling cash flow crisis, particularly for small and medium enterprises borrowing at double-digit interest rates?\n\nGiven that the small and medium enterprises - SMEs - are essential for export diversification and growth, what special provisions are being made to protect them from insolvency due to a VAT refund delay, ensuring they could continue to operate and contribute to the 2030 goal set by the President?\n\n2. Given that exporters are now effectively financing the Treasury interest-free, will the Ministry introduce an interim digital mechanism to guarantee timely refunds until a fully transparent VAT system is operational? That is, either through a direct VAT live operation or if not, try to see as to how you could bring in this temporary measure.\n\n3. How does the Ministry reconcile the declared target of US Dollars 36 billion in exports by 2030 with a policy that industry leaders unanimously warn will discourage investments, delay operations and erode competitiveness? Will this lead to a case of defeating or discouraging President Anura Kumara Dissanayake\u2019s targets and objectives?\n\n4. What safeguards or transitional support mechanisms would be established to protect small and medium enterprises and deemed exporters who supply larger exporters but cannot survive prolonged refund delays from collapse due to blocked working capital?\n\n5. What is the projected financial benefit to the Treasury from abolishing SVAT, and how does the Ministry justify such short-term gains against the far greater foreign exchange losses and employment risks from weakened exports and company closures?"
